{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with precision, tension, and emotional depth. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie transcends the typical biopic genre by transforming a story about code and connectivity into a gripping drama about ambition, betrayal, and isolation. the pacing is relentless, with sorkin’s rapid-fire dialogue propelling the narrative forward like a legal thriller disguised as a tech origin story.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character’s brilliant yet emotionally stunted persona with chilling accuracy. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a man who is socially awkward, fiercely intelligent, and tragically unable to connect on a human level. the supporting cast is equally strong: andrew garfield as eduardo saverin brings heart and vulnerability to the role of zuckerberg’s once-loyal friend, while jesse plemons and armie hammer bring quiet menace and charisma to their roles as the winklevoss twins, respectively.\n\nthe cinematography by jeff cronenweth is stark and cool, reflecting the sterile, digital world the characters inhabit. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and minimalist, using electronic textures to mirror the cold logic of technology and the emotional void at the center of the story. every frame feels intentional, every cut precise.\n\nwhat makes the film truly remarkable is its ability to explore complex themes — intellectual property, friendship, class, and the cost of innovation — without ever becoming didactic. the courtroom scenes, framed as flashbacks, serve not just as narrative devices but as metaphors for the moral ambiguity surrounding success. the film never clearly sides with any character, instead forcing the viewer to grapple with the consequences of ambition and the erosion of trust.\n\nthe social network is more than a movie about facebook; it’s a meditation on the modern condition — how we build connections through technology while losing the ability to form real ones. it’s sharp, intelligent, and deeply unsettling. by the end, you’re not sure whether to admire zuckerberg’s genius or pity his loneliness. either way, you’re left with a profound sense of unease about the world we’ve built — and the people who built it.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014625910262111574}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -4.410734163684538e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00043156370520591736}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4582989513874054}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.252384752035141}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0010349161457270384}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.003972972277551889}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011789103882620111}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.1295183897018433}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.006728847976773977}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.5502323508262634}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.207999518257566e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00028951745480298996}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.48412784934043884}, {"token": " precision", "logprob": -0.9521429538726807}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " tension", "logprob": -0.49507957696914673}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " emotional", "logprob": -0.06920499354600906}, {"token": " depth", "logprob": -0.07009072601795197}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -3.933898824470816e-06}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.0002809368306770921}, {"to